# 28. Dependency Rules

The repository SHALL enforce dependency direction.

Allowed:

```text
kernel-cordis → kernel-api
store-sqlite → events
cognitive-ruvector → cognitive-api
cognitive-rvf → cognitive-api
react → client
ui-astryx → react
ag-ui → events/client
domainforge → kernel-api/projections
realitytrace → events/evidence contracts
profiles/* → packages/*
profile → kernel-api (types only)
```

Forbidden examples:

```text
kernel-api → cordis
kernel-api → ruvector
kernel-api → react
kernel-api → astryx
kernel-api → ag-ui
kernel-api → domainforge
kernel-api → realitytrace

events → ruvector
cognitive-api → ruvector
client → react
react → astryx
```

Dependency-cycle checking SHOULD be part of CI.

---

# 29. Reference Profiles

## 29.1 Copilot Profile

The Copilot profile SHALL demonstrate that common Copilot-style behavior can be constructed without introducing profile-specific kernel primitives.

Composition:

```text
runtime
  +
runs
  +
continuations
  +
frontend capabilities
  +
state projections
  +
artifact projections
  +
AG-UI
  +
React bindings
  +
Astryx UI
```

Acceptance behaviors:

* streamed agent response;
* frontend tool;
* backend tool;
* shared application state;
* human approval;
* artifact rendering;
* generative UI projection;
* resumable interaction;
* AG-UI-compatible event output.

---

## 29.2 Harness Profile

The Harness profile SHALL demonstrate:

* model providers;
* tool registry;
* agent loop;
* sessions;
* storage;
* skills/extensions;
* scheduler;
* sandbox adapters;
* replaceable services;
* dynamic activation/deactivation;
* subagent composition.

A provider MUST be replaceable without consumers importing its concrete implementation.

Removal of a capability MUST deterministically deactivate dependent fibers.

Restoration of a compatible capability MAY reactivate them.

---

# 30. Cognitive Reference Scenario

Given a support case:

```text
duplicate payment
customer = X
amount = Y
current state = investigation
```

The runtime creates a cognitive query.

RuVector may return:

* semantically related DomainForge objects;
* similar prior cases;
* related artifacts;
* relevant RealityTrace executions;
* previously useful tools;
* analogous resolutions.

Returned objects contain source references.

The Framework fetches authoritative source data and filters according to context and policy before supplying it to the consuming agent.

If multiple eligible tools/providers can satisfy an operation:

```text
semantic constraints
      ↓
security constraints
      ↓
capability compatibility
      ↓
eligible candidates
      ↓
RuVector/SONA ranking
      ↓
selection
```

The selection and outcome are recorded so later adaptation has explicit evidence.

---

# 31. Failure Semantics

## 31.1 Cordis/kernel-engine failure

Must not corrupt the durable event store.

Fiber teardown SHALL attempt all registered disposers even where an earlier disposer fails.

Aggregate cleanup errors SHOULD be observable.

## 31.2 SQLite failure

Durable operations SHALL fail closed.

The runtime MUST NOT claim a durable event was committed before SQLite confirms the transaction.

## 31.3 RuVector failure

Framework execution continues without cognitive optimization unless an application explicitly declares cognition as required.

Fallback:

```text
RuVector unavailable
      ↓
deterministic/non-cognitive behavior
```

## 31.4 RVF failure

Failure to create a cognitive snapshot MUST NOT corrupt the active cognitive index or event store.

## 31.5 DomainForge integration failure

Components requiring unavailable semantic projections wait/fail according to their declared requirement.

No `.sea` fallback grammar is introduced.

## 31.6 RealityTrace failure

Framework execution MAY continue in normal mode.

Applications requiring trace evidence for a regulated/high-assurance operation MAY opt into fail-closed policy outside the generic kernel.

## 31.7 UI failure

UI rendering failure MUST NOT mutate or corrupt authoritative runtime state.

## 31.8 Protocol adapter failure

Reconnect/resume SHOULD use durable run/event position rather than assuming the stream remained continuously connected.

---

# 32. Security Model

Capabilities SHALL default to no authority. A Context carries an authenticated principal and tenant/workspace scope as separate values from its capability visibility. A child Context may narrow authority but MUST NOT gain a permission by inheriting or discovering a capability. Server-side policy is checked at invocation and again before an irreversible external commitment; a client projection or model/tool declaration is never an authorization decision.

Every capability invocation SHALL have access to:

* caller identity/context;
* scope;
* capability identity;
* selected provider;
* applicable policy information;
* trace/correlation identity.

Sensitive cognitive retrieval SHALL enforce authorization independently of similarity.

The retrieval path SHALL constrain candidates by tenant/scope before disclosing metadata where supported, and SHALL authorize each authoritative dereference before returning content. Unauthorized hits MUST NOT leak titles, embeddings, counts, or provenance that would reveal restricted objects. An imported or detached RVF remains untrusted data and cannot grant capabilities or bypass these checks.

Cognitive indexes SHOULD avoid embedding unrestricted sensitive payloads when references or redacted projections are sufficient.

External commitments SHOULD use idempotency identifiers when the target supports them.

Protocol input MUST be schema validated.

Untrusted protobuf/JSON payloads MUST NOT construct arbitrary runtime components without authorization.

Each external request SHALL bind authenticated actor, requested operation, idempotency key, and causation identity. The server MUST enforce size/depth limits and deadlines on untrusted payloads and streams. Credentials SHALL remain in provider-specific secret storage and MUST NOT be serialized into events, artifacts, traces, cognitive entries, or RVF snapshots. Audit records SHALL include policy decision and principal reference without storing secret values.

Applications SHALL define retention and erasure policy before storing personal or regulated content. Immutable events should carry minimal metadata and references to separately erasable encrypted payloads where deletion may be required. Erasure removes or cryptographically destroys eligible payloads, records a tombstone and legal reason, and invalidates/rebuilds derived indexes and snapshots; the remaining event envelope preserves causality without preserving erased content. Backups and exported RVF files require a documented retention/revocation policy. Replay after erasure reports a redacted gap instead of reconstructing deleted data.

---

# 33. Observability and Provenance

Every significant execution SHOULD preserve enough identifiers to reconstruct causality:

```text
correlation
    │
run
    │
event
    │
causation
    │
component/fiber
    │
capability/provider
    │
semantic reference
    │
artifact
    │
RealityTrace reference
```

Logging is not sufficient.

Machine-readable causal identity is required.

---

# 34. Testing Strategy

## 34.1 Kernel unit tests

Test:

* context inheritance;
* capability resolution;
* scope isolation;
* component lifecycle;
* continuation lifecycle;
* event ordering;
* external commitment handling.

## 34.2 Property-based tests

Use `fast-check`.

Generate arbitrary:

* component graphs;
* dependency graphs;
* capability appearance/removal;
* activation sequences;
* disposal sequences;
* continuation sequences;
* provider replacement;
* failures.

Critical properties:

### Reversibility

For a purely reversible component:

```text
state0
  ↓ mount
state1
  ↓ unmount
state2
```

Observable `state2` MUST equal `state0` with respect to the component's owned effects.

### Dependency closure

A component MUST NOT remain active when a required capability is unavailable.

### Scope isolation

A child scope MUST NOT leak isolated capabilities into siblings or parents.

### Disposal idempotence

Repeated disposal MUST NOT duplicate teardown side effects.

### Replay determinism

Given the same authoritative event sequence and compatible projection version, deterministic projections MUST produce equivalent results.

### Cognitive non-authority

Deleting or replacing the cognitive index MUST NOT change deterministic authorization outcomes.

### Adaptive containment

Adaptive ranking MUST NOT select an ineligible candidate.

---

# 35. Compatibility Testing

CopilotKit and DeepSeek Harness SHOULD appear only in compatibility/reference testing where useful.

They SHOULD NOT be runtime requirements.

The Framework SHALL test scenarios behaviorally.

Example:

```text
scenario
   ├── reference Copilot-style implementation
   └── Framework Copilot profile
```

Compare semantic behavior rather than requiring identical internal implementation.

For Harness behavior:

```text
provide capability A
      ↓
dependent component activates
      ↓
remove A
      ↓
dependent component deactivates
      ↓
restore compatible A
      ↓
dependent component reactivates
```

The Cordis paper and DeepSeek Harness serve as the theoretical/reference basis for these composition semantics.

---

# 36. End-to-End Tests

Use Playwright for UI journeys.

Initial E2E journey:

```text
launch Bun application
      ↓
load DomainForge-derived semantic projection
      ↓
mount runtime components
      ↓
open Astryx UI
      ↓
start agent run
      ↓
invoke backend capability
      ↓
stream result to UI
      ↓
open continuation
      ↓
human resolves continuation
      ↓
run resumes
      ↓
artifact produced
      ↓
artifact opened
      ↓
events persisted
      ↓
RealityTrace observation emitted
      ↓
RuVector indexes completed interaction
      ↓
new related run recalls prior interaction
      ↓
RVF snapshot created
```

The test SHALL verify authoritative state independently from displayed UI state.

---

# 37. Initial Implementation Sequence

## Phase 0 — Architecture characterization

Before implementation:

* characterize Cordis lifecycle behavior;
* characterize Bun 1.4 runtime features used;
* prove Bun + selected Cordis package compatibility;
* prove selected RuVector bindings under Bun;
* test the selected RuVector Bun native binding and identify a documented fallback where one exists; do not assume the RVF package has a Bun WASM fallback;
* spike Connect handling over `Bun.serve`, including the pinned `@bufbuild/protobuf` and `@connectrpc/connect` runtime plus compatible Buf/`protoc-gen-es`/Connect code-generation tuple;
* characterize existing DomainForge integration interface;
* characterize existing RealityTrace integration interface.

Phase 0 SHALL produce an exact dependency/version matrix, runnable compatibility fixtures, and a decision record for each boundary. Core fixtures include: Cordis activation, dependency loss, partial setup failure and reverse disposal; DomainForge Graph parsing with source identity under Bun; SQLite transaction/crash and optimistic append; Connect unary and streaming over `Bun.serve`; and RuVector index rebuild and fallback. Optional adapters require their own fixtures before release: RVF Bun load/export/import/branch isolation; RealityTrace CLI enrollment-to-observation with schema-valid correlation and duplicate delivery; SONA candidate filtering, ranking, feedback persistence, and deterministic fallback; and SemanticPack decoding/version handling.

If a required fixture fails, the affected adapter is blocked or replaced behind its framework-owned interface. RVF, adaptive ranking, and RealityTrace delivery are optional capabilities and SHALL NOT block a minimal kernel/event runtime. If Cordis fails its required lifecycle fixture, the reference kernel engine choice must be revised before Phase 1; the public kernel contract stays fixed. Record unsupported capabilities explicitly rather than substituting an untested API.

Core application features should begin only after the core Phase 0 gates pass. Optional adapters may remain blocked and absent from the released capability matrix.

## Phase 1 — Kernel

Implement:

* `kernel-api`;
* Context;
* Capability;
* Component;
* Scope;
* Effect;
* Fiber abstraction;
* kernel-Cordis mapping.

Success criterion:

A component graph can activate/deactivate dynamically and reverse owned effects deterministically.

## Phase 2 — Durable event system

Implement:

* event envelopes;
* in-memory event store;
* Bun SQLite store;
* projection checkpoints;
* replay.

Success criterion:

Delete all derived state and reconstruct it from SQLite.

## Phase 3 — Continuations and commitments

Implement:

* continuation lifecycle;
* resume/cancel/expire;
* external commitment/outbox model;
* idempotency mechanism.

## Phase 4 — Protobuf + Connect

Implement canonical schemas and Bun transport.

Success criterion:

A second process can observe and control a runtime through generated protobuf clients.

## Phase 5 — DomainForge + RealityTrace

Implement adapters without modifying either project.

Success criterion:

A semantic object can be correlated through:

```text
DomainForge
→ SemanticRef
→ runtime execution
→ event
→ queued RealityTrace observation
```

The test SHALL also verify SXR workflow prerequisites, accepted receipt, duplicate retry behavior, and the distinction between observation, derived evidence, and settlement. A failed or unavailable SXR adapter SHALL leave the Framework event committed and expose delivery status.

## Phase 6 — Cognitive API + RuVector

Implement:

* `CognitiveIndex`;
* memory implementation;
* RuVector adapter;
* indexing projection;
* semantic recall;
* authoritative source dereferencing.

Success criterion:

The RuVector index can be deleted and reconstructed without changing deterministic runtime behavior.

## Phase 7 — Optional RVF

Implement only after the Phase 0 RVF gate passes:

* export;
* snapshot;
* branch;
* lineage metadata;
* import classification.

Success criterion:

A cognitive branch can diverge without mutating authoritative application state. If the gate fails, skip this phase and record RVF as unsupported in the release capability matrix.

## Phase 8 — Headless client + React + Astryx

Implement:

* client;
* React bindings;
* Astryx reference surfaces.

## Phase 9 — AG-UI profile

Implement event mappings and demonstrate existing AG-UI interoperability.

## Phase 10 — Copilot and Harness reference profiles

Demonstrate that both profiles are compositions rather than kernel exceptions.

Implement the `@cognate/cli` `create` and `dev` commands, the starter template, package scripts, planned documentation, and runnable examples claimed by the README. The acceptance fixture generates a project in a clean directory, installs its pinned packages, runs `bun dev`, executes a semantic projection, survives restart, and passes `bun test`. The CLI MUST report unsupported optional adapters rather than generating imports for packages that failed Phase 0.

---

# 38. V0 Dependency Policy

## Core

Aim for the smallest practical set:

```text
Bun 1.4.x
Cordis
@bufbuild/protobuf
@connectrpc/connect
```

The exact `cordis` prerelease and Connect/protobuf runtime versions SHALL be pinned only after Phase 0 fixtures pass. A runtime library alone does not supply a Bun server adapter or generated service stubs.

## Cognitive implementation

```text
selected RuVector packages
@ruvector/rvf / appropriate RVF binding
```

RVF is an optional dependency. The minimal `CognitiveIndex` works without snapshot or branch support; those capabilities are discoverable only when the adapter passes its fixtures.

Prefer narrow RuVector packages over the umbrella package where feasible.

## UI

```text
react
react-dom
@astryxdesign/core
@astryxdesign/theme-neutral
```

Include StyleX only where required by the selected Astryx distribution path.

## Optional interoperability

```text
@ag-ui/core
@ag-ui/proto or encoder as required
MCP SDK
A2A implementation
provider SDKs
```

## Development

```text
fast-check
playwright
@bufbuild/buf (or a pinned Buf CLI)
@bufbuild/protoc-gen-es
Connect code-generation plugin compatible with the selected runtime
```

The Phase 0 generator fixture SHALL compile the root `proto/` sources twice to byte-identical `packages/proto/` output and verify generated client/server code against the pinned runtime. The [Connect generator documentation](https://www.npmjs.com/package/%40connectrpc/protoc-gen-connect-es) lists a Connect plugin, but its release version must be checked against the selected runtime before it is adopted.

Use `bun:test` rather than introducing another general test runner unless a concrete limitation requires one.

---

# 39. Dependency Maturity Policy

Cordis, Astryx, RuVector and RVF are active external projects.

The Framework SHALL therefore:

* pin exact tested versions;
* update through controlled dependency PRs;
* maintain compatibility tests around behavioral contracts;
* prevent third-party types from leaking across public abstraction boundaries;
* maintain fallback implementations for core interfaces;
* avoid depending on undocumented internals unless isolated inside one adapter;
* record the exact dependency versions used to produce releases.

RuVector and RVF in particular are evolving rapidly; the Framework gains their functionality without making their implementation architecture part of its own identity.

---

# 40. Architectural Acceptance Criteria

The architecture is considered validated only if all of the following are true.

### A. Cordis can be removed conceptually

Application code imports no Cordis runtime types.

### B. RuVector can be deleted operationally

Deleting cognitive state does not destroy truth or authorization state.

### C. `.sea` does not know the Framework exists

No framework-specific grammar is required.

### D. DomainForge remains independently releasable

Framework integration is an adapter.

### E. RealityTrace remains independently releasable

Framework integration is an evidence adapter.

### F. Astryx can be replaced

Headless and React APIs remain valid.

### G. Connect can be replaced

The service contract survives transport changes.

### H. AG-UI can be removed

Native Framework execution and UI remain functional.

### I. Learning cannot expand authority

Adaptive systems operate only inside deterministic eligibility.

### J. A Copilot-like application can be composed

No Copilot-specific kernel primitives are introduced.

### K. A Harness-like system can be composed

No Harness-specific kernel primitives are introduced.

### L. The cognitive system can improve without semantic mutation

New learned rankings and memories do not require `.sea` grammar changes.

### M. Runtime behavior is causally inspectable

Events, fibers, capabilities, semantic references and RealityTrace observations can be correlated.

---

# 41. Definition of the Framework

The Framework can be summarized as:

> A Bun-native semantic application substrate in which dynamically composed capabilities execute through scoped, reversible lifecycles; durable facts form an authoritative event history; unfinished work is represented explicitly as continuations; DomainForge projects existing `.sea` semantics into the runtime without language changes; an optional RealityTrace adapter submits correlated observations into its evidence workflow; RuVector provides rebuildable cognitive recall and bounded adaptive ranking; optional RVF support provides portable cognitive state and branching after Bun compatibility is proven; Protobuf and Connect expose typed cross-language boundaries; and headless clients, AG-UI, React and Astryx project the same underlying system into user-facing applications.

The authority and projection paths are:

```text
.sea → DomainForge Graph → versioned semantic projection
                              │
                              ▼
                      Framework execution
                              │
                              ▼
                    SQLite event history
                      │       │       │
                      │       │       └→ optional RealityTrace observations
                      │       └→ RuVector derived cognition → optional RVF
                      └→ client/protocol projections → React / Astryx / AG-UI / Connect
```

The architectural rule that governs the entire design is:

> **Semantics constrain execution. Events preserve framework history. Cognition discovers relevance. RealityTrace derives evidence from qualified observations. Representations remain replaceable.**
